00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:01.860
There's a thing called the Monster Group

00:00:01.860 --> 00:00:04.400
which is a beautiful, very large,

00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:08.519
symmetrical thing. 
- We're gonna have a

00:00:08.519 --> 00:00:10.450
look at what's called the monster group.

00:00:10.450 --> 00:00:12.900
(Brady: That's a cool name isn't it?)
- It's a really really cool name, yeah.

00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:15.089
There's never been any kind of

00:00:15.089 --> 00:00:18.029
explanation of why it's there and it's

00:00:18.029 --> 00:00:21.539
obviously not there just by coincidence.

00:00:21.539 --> 00:00:24.390
Okay, so groups are algebraic structures

00:00:24.390 --> 00:00:26.400
that arise naturally in the study of

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:29.130
symmetry. Now that can be symmetry in a

00:00:29.130 --> 00:00:30.210
sort of a vast range of different

00:00:30.210 --> 00:00:32.640
contexts. Let's take a geometric- familiar

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:34.380
sort of geometric shapes, so let's take an

00:00:34.380 --> 00:00:36.180
equilateral triangle, okay and let's

00:00:36.180 --> 00:00:38.760
label the vertices 1, 2 &amp; 3. I want to

00:00:38.760 --> 00:00:39.750
consider the symmetries of an

00:00:39.750 --> 00:00:41.550
equilateral triangle, so what what I'm

00:00:41.550 --> 00:00:43.500
really thinking about here are rotations

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:44.910
and reflections. Ok, so these are natural

00:00:44.910 --> 00:00:46.770
symmetries of this triangle. For example,

00:00:46.770 --> 00:00:49.410
we can consider certain rotations about

00:00:49.410 --> 00:00:50.309
that centre point.

00:00:50.309 --> 00:00:52.379
Okay now let's let's write down this

00:00:52.379 --> 00:00:53.850
these collections of symmetries, let's

00:00:53.850 --> 00:00:55.949
call this collection G. If I sort of

00:00:55.949 --> 00:00:57.660
think of putting a pin in the paper at

00:00:57.660 --> 00:00:59.670
that centre point and then moving the

00:00:59.670 --> 00:01:01.350
triangle around; well I can rotate

00:01:01.350 --> 00:01:03.359
through certain angles and the triangle

00:01:03.359 --> 00:01:05.280
will move back onto itself. Ok I have to

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:06.780
be careful which angle I choose here but-

00:01:06.780 --> 00:01:08.250
sort of, it's not too difficult to see

00:01:08.250 --> 00:01:10.830
that I could say rotate through 120

00:01:10.830 --> 00:01:13.290
degrees - let's write that R120 - that

00:01:13.290 --> 00:01:15.509
would have the effect of permuting those

00:01:15.509 --> 00:01:17.549
vertices, the corners of the triangle. And

00:01:17.549 --> 00:01:19.950
I could also go through say 240 degrees,

00:01:19.950 --> 00:01:21.090
and they're really the only two

00:01:21.090 --> 00:01:22.590
non-trivial rotations that I can do

00:01:22.590 --> 00:01:23.970
which will move that triangle back onto

00:01:23.970 --> 00:01:26.220
itself. 
- (Because 360 doesn't count?)

00:01:26.220 --> 00:01:27.960
Well 360 does count actually and that's

00:01:27.960 --> 00:01:29.100
really an important one so let's

00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:30.150
actually include that one, we're gonna

00:01:30.150 --> 00:01:31.979
need there to get this idea of a group,

00:01:31.979 --> 00:01:33.810
so let's call that R0. That means do

00:01:33.810 --> 00:01:35.220
nothing, okay, so that's the do nothing

00:01:35.220 --> 00:01:36.659
symmetry that's really really important

00:01:36.659 --> 00:01:38.280
in the concept of a group. Ok so we've

00:01:38.280 --> 00:01:40.290
got our rotations now what else have we

00:01:40.290 --> 00:01:41.460
got? Let's consider sort of mirror

00:01:41.460 --> 00:01:43.950
symmetry. Okay so we could think about

00:01:43.950 --> 00:01:46.979
putting a mirror down that line and that

00:01:46.979 --> 00:01:48.509
would also give us a symmetry of the of

00:01:48.509 --> 00:01:50.700
the triangle. Ok, it would fix the top

00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:53.100
vertex and it would swap these two bottom
corners.

00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:55.799
Ok so let's call that a, so let's include

00:01:55.799 --> 00:01:57.570
that over here, and then similarly we

00:01:57.570 --> 00:02:01.469
have b and c. Ok so we've got three lines

00:02:01.469 --> 00:02:03.000
of reflectional symmetry of this

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:05.820
triangle as well; ok so a, b and c.
So what

00:02:05.820 --> 00:02:07.020
I've written down here,
these are- it's not

00:02:07.020 --> 00:02:08.610
too difficult to check that this is this

00:02:08.610 --> 00:02:10.709
is a complete set of symmetries of the

00:02:10.709 --> 00:02:12.120
equilateral triangle, we've got six of them

00:02:12.120 --> 00:02:12.910
in total.

00:02:12.910 --> 00:02:13.750
This is what's called the symmetry group

00:02:13.750 --> 00:02:16.150
of the equilateral triangle. The key

00:02:16.150 --> 00:02:17.560
concept with a group is it's more than

00:02:17.560 --> 00:02:19.510
just a collection of symmetries. There's

00:02:19.510 --> 00:02:21.850
a rule or there's an operation which

00:02:21.850 --> 00:02:23.350
allows us to combine two symmetries

00:02:23.350 --> 00:02:25.180
together. So if we take two symmetries we

00:02:25.180 --> 00:02:27.130
could- we could take the first one and

00:02:27.130 --> 00:02:28.840
combine it with the second and that will

00:02:28.840 --> 00:02:32.240
give us a third symmetry of the triangle.
- (How does that work?)

00:02:32.240 --> 00:02:34.510
Okay let me give you an example. So let's

00:02:34.510 --> 00:02:36.400
say consider- let's draw this triangle

00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:38.560
out again a little bit smaller so I've got

00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:39.460
our 1, 2, 3.

00:02:39.460 --> 00:02:41.770
Okay let's actually see what happens to

00:02:41.770 --> 00:02:43.120
the vertices when we apply
these symmetries;

00:02:43.120 --> 00:02:50.200
so let's say apply- let's do a, okay. Okay

00:02:50.200 --> 00:02:52.600
so a, we defined it to be this reflection

00:02:52.600 --> 00:02:54.340
in this vertical line of symmetry here.

00:02:54.340 --> 00:02:56.500
So what does that do to the do to the

00:02:56.500 --> 00:02:58.960
vertices? Well it swaps the bottom two,

00:02:58.960 --> 00:03:00.910
okay, so it looks like that. That's what

00:03:00.910 --> 00:03:02.050
would that will look like after we've

00:03:02.050 --> 00:03:04.360
applied that reflectional symmetry. And

00:03:04.360 --> 00:03:05.800
let's do another one, let's now follow

00:03:05.800 --> 00:03:08.770
that up with say our anti-clockwise

00:03:08.770 --> 00:03:10.270
rotation through 120

00:03:10.270 --> 00:03:12.460
degrees. Okay now what does that give us?

00:03:12.460 --> 00:03:14.410
Okay so what does that mean? So this

00:03:14.410 --> 00:03:18.100
becomes 2, this is 1, and this is 3. Ok so

00:03:18.100 --> 00:03:19.720
that's what we've got. Now we could have

00:03:19.720 --> 00:03:22.150
gone straight from the initial triangle

00:03:22.150 --> 00:03:24.310
to this end triangle with just one

00:03:24.310 --> 00:03:25.810
operation, what would that operation be?

00:03:25.810 --> 00:03:27.730
Well what have we done here? We've fixed

00:03:27.730 --> 00:03:29.440
the bottom right vertex 3, we've

00:03:29.440 --> 00:03:31.930
swapped these two. Well that corresponds

00:03:31.930 --> 00:03:34.240
to what we called c. Okay, that's that

00:03:34.240 --> 00:03:36.430
reflection so we've got c. Okay so what

00:03:36.430 --> 00:03:37.420
we could do we could write that down

00:03:37.420 --> 00:03:41.200
sort of mathematically, R120 composed

00:03:41.200 --> 00:03:44.410
with a equals c. What we're trying to do

00:03:44.410 --> 00:03:45.940
here is we're trying to define a sort of

00:03:45.940 --> 00:03:48.459
like a multiplication on this set. Okay

00:03:48.459 --> 00:03:49.660
so it's like a- like some sort of

00:03:49.660 --> 00:03:51.459
natural operation which allows us to

00:03:51.459 --> 00:03:52.720
combine these two symmetries together

00:03:52.720 --> 00:03:54.640
and get another one. That's really, that's

00:03:54.640 --> 00:03:55.840
really what's going on and that's really

00:03:55.840 --> 00:03:57.220
the key idea of a group. It's a

00:03:57.220 --> 00:03:58.930
collection of objects with some

00:03:58.930 --> 00:04:01.330
operation defined on it which satisfies

00:04:01.330 --> 00:04:03.040
these sort of properties, this allows-

00:04:03.040 --> 00:04:04.690
this allows us to define an

00:04:04.690 --> 00:04:06.280
abstract notion of a group. So this

00:04:06.280 --> 00:04:08.380
object here this is the set, this is the

00:04:08.380 --> 00:04:10.209
collection of symmetries. Ok, now the

00:04:10.209 --> 00:04:12.400
group, strictly speaking, is this

00:04:12.400 --> 00:04:14.530
collection of symmetries together with

00:04:14.530 --> 00:04:16.120
this rule for combining symmetries

00:04:16.120 --> 00:04:18.489
together. We can

00:04:18.489 --> 00:04:20.199
perhaps try and understand groups by

00:04:20.199 --> 00:04:23.680
breaking them up into smaller pieces. The

00:04:23.680 --> 00:04:25.180
analogy to keep in mind here is the idea

00:04:25.180 --> 00:04:28.449
of factorising a positive number into

00:04:28.449 --> 00:04:30.009
prime factors, okay, so this should be

00:04:30.009 --> 00:04:32.289
familiar to everybody. Okay. now we can do

00:04:32.289 --> 00:04:33.940
something like that for a group; it's a

00:04:33.940 --> 00:04:35.020
little bit more subtle, it's a little bit

00:04:35.020 --> 00:04:37.720
more complicated. 
- Let's say 60 is 2 times

00:04:37.720 --> 00:04:41.110
2 times 3 times 5; and those numbers I

00:04:41.110 --> 00:04:43.120
just said were all prime numbers. In

00:04:43.120 --> 00:04:46.180
group theory there's a sort of

00:04:46.180 --> 00:04:48.220
similar-sounding theorem that every

00:04:48.220 --> 00:04:50.289
group is composed of a number of prime

00:04:50.289 --> 00:04:52.449
groups, except the term is simple groups.

00:04:52.449 --> 00:04:54.759
They're not actually terribly simple.

00:04:54.759 --> 00:04:57.099
Let's just consider, let me call R here,

00:04:57.099 --> 00:04:59.289
let's just consider the rotations, ok, so

00:04:59.289 --> 00:05:00.610
let's forget about the reflections for a

00:05:00.610 --> 00:05:03.190
second. Ok, now as I said you can talk

00:05:03.190 --> 00:05:04.509
about that you have this notion of an

00:05:04.509 --> 00:05:07.060
abstract group. Now R itself is actually

00:05:07.060 --> 00:05:09.490
a group, ok? It's not the symmetry group

00:05:09.490 --> 00:05:10.870
of the equilateral triangle it's the

00:05:10.870 --> 00:05:12.849
rotation- it's the group of rotational

00:05:12.849 --> 00:05:15.499
symmetries of the equilateral triangle.
- (Like a subset?)

00:05:15.499 --> 00:05:17.380
It's like a subset but it's what we call

00:05:17.380 --> 00:05:19.000
a subgroup. And you see it's not just

00:05:19.000 --> 00:05:20.560
any old subset, the key property is that

00:05:20.560 --> 00:05:21.849
it- first of all it has the do-nothing

00:05:21.849 --> 00:05:24.039
element, that's really important, and the

00:05:24.039 --> 00:05:25.240
other key property we're looking for here

00:05:25.240 --> 00:05:27.669
is that it's- this set is closed under

00:05:27.669 --> 00:05:29.560
this operation that we defined. In other

00:05:29.560 --> 00:05:31.360
words if I take two rotations in here

00:05:31.360 --> 00:05:33.250
and combine them then I get another

00:05:33.250 --> 00:05:35.409
element of that particular set. I can't

00:05:35.409 --> 00:05:37.210
combine two rotations together and then

00:05:37.210 --> 00:05:38.680
suddenly get a reflection, that's not

00:05:38.680 --> 00:05:40.000
going to happen, we can easily check that.

00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:42.009
Ok so that's the key property that this

00:05:42.009 --> 00:05:44.500
is actually a subgroup. Ok, so that's R

00:05:44.500 --> 00:05:47.349
and let's define S. To take- ok, we're

00:05:47.349 --> 00:05:48.610
gonna have R0 in there, we're going to

00:05:48.610 --> 00:05:50.259
need R0 in any of our- in any of our

00:05:50.259 --> 00:05:52.210
subgroups that's this key do-nothing 
element, and

00:05:52.210 --> 00:05:54.219
let's just take a. Now this has got three

00:05:54.219 --> 00:05:55.870
elements and this has got two elements,

00:05:55.870 --> 00:05:58.389
and again this set S is a subgroup

00:05:58.389 --> 00:06:00.789
because if you do- if you compose a with

00:06:00.789 --> 00:06:03.130
itself that's a reflection done twice

00:06:03.130 --> 00:06:04.599
but that gets you R0, that's the do

00:06:04.599 --> 00:06:08.151
nothing- that's the do nothing operation.
- (So to be a subgroup it always has)

00:06:08.151 --> 00:06:10.559
(to hav- to leave you a method
to get back home?)

00:06:10.559 --> 00:06:12.789
Yeah that's right, so it must be- 
yeah that's

00:06:12.789 --> 00:06:14.050
right. So I mean it must contain the do

00:06:14.050 --> 00:06:15.580
nothing element and it must have the

00:06:15.580 --> 00:06:17.050
property that if I take any two elements

00:06:17.050 --> 00:06:18.940
in that smaller subset, if I combine them,

00:06:18.940 --> 00:06:20.500
then I stay inside that set, that's the

00:06:20.500 --> 00:06:22.060
key thing we're looking for here. It's

00:06:22.060 --> 00:06:24.069
not too hard to see that we actually can

00:06:24.069 --> 00:06:27.310
write G as R times S as a sort of a

00:06:27.310 --> 00:06:29.620
factorisation. What this means is,

00:06:29.620 --> 00:06:31.150
what I've written down here, is

00:06:31.150 --> 00:06:32.060
that every element

00:06:32.060 --> 00:06:34.820
of this of this symmetry group G is a

00:06:34.820 --> 00:06:36.710
product of something in R with

00:06:36.710 --> 00:06:38.510
something in S. Okay so that's why we

00:06:38.510 --> 00:06:39.950
might say that this is a factorisation

00:06:39.950 --> 00:06:42.920
of G into two smaller pieces. Okay so

00:06:42.920 --> 00:06:44.360
these things are what are called simple

00:06:44.360 --> 00:06:46.400
groups. So the idea would be then to try

00:06:46.400 --> 00:06:47.960
and understand all groups, or in

00:06:47.960 --> 00:06:49.250
other words to try to understand all

00:06:49.250 --> 00:06:51.530
possible types of symmetry. Can we give a

00:06:51.530 --> 00:06:52.940
complete description of what all these

00:06:52.940 --> 00:06:55.070
basic building blocks, all these atoms of

00:06:55.070 --> 00:06:56.590
symmetry, what they actually look like?

00:06:56.590 --> 00:06:58.690
Okay so this has been sort of a

00:06:58.690 --> 00:07:01.250
fundamental problem in group theory and in

00:07:01.250 --> 00:07:02.720
algebra for more than a hundred years.

00:07:02.720 --> 00:07:05.210
What's remarkable is that it is actually

00:07:05.210 --> 00:07:06.800
possible to do this, okay, so there's this

00:07:06.800 --> 00:07:07.850
there's a theorem called the

00:07:07.850 --> 00:07:09.280
Classification of Finite Simple Groups

00:07:09.280 --> 00:07:12.410
which was announced around 1980 which

00:07:12.410 --> 00:07:14.810
provides us with this sort of 
periodic table

00:07:14.810 --> 00:07:18.280
of symmetry. 
- (I can see it behind you!)

00:07:18.280 --> 00:07:19.610
That's right it's up on the

00:07:19.610 --> 00:07:21.770
wall there, that's right. 
Okay so what- what is

00:07:21.770 --> 00:07:24.110
this theorem then? So it's it's um it's

00:07:24.110 --> 00:07:25.820
this incredible amazing result

00:07:25.820 --> 00:07:28.340
that describes what all the finite

00:07:28.340 --> 00:07:30.110
simple groups look like. So this is a

00:07:30.110 --> 00:07:32.960
really very unique theorem in all of

00:07:32.960 --> 00:07:35.170
mathematics, I mean it was a huge

00:07:35.170 --> 00:07:37.340
international collaborative effort to

00:07:37.340 --> 00:07:39.260
prove this theorem over many many decades.

00:07:39.260 --> 00:07:42.770
Its proof runs to between 10,000 and

00:07:42.770 --> 00:07:48.920
100,000 pages and it was done by three

00:07:48.920 --> 00:07:51.650
or four hundred mathematicians, I don't

00:07:51.650 --> 00:07:53.630
think anybody's read through the whole

00:07:53.630 --> 00:07:56.660
proof.
- It's sort of an incredible

00:07:56.660 --> 00:07:58.310
incredible achievement really,

00:07:58.310 --> 00:07:59.480
certainly one of the highlights of 20th

00:07:59.480 --> 00:08:01.700
century mathematics.
- There were some

00:08:01.700 --> 00:08:05.180
mistakes in it; in anything as long as

00:08:05.180 --> 00:08:06.710
that they are bound to be some mistakes

00:08:06.710 --> 00:08:08.420
so it's not terribly worrying that 
there are

00:08:08.420 --> 00:08:10.130
some mistakes in it.
- What we're looking

00:08:10.130 --> 00:08:11.150
to do is we're just trying to sort of

00:08:11.150 --> 00:08:14.840
identify various families of simple

00:08:14.840 --> 00:08:16.520
groups. So for example one family will be

00:08:16.520 --> 00:08:18.080
all of the groups which have size 'a

00:08:18.080 --> 00:08:20.060
prime number'. Okay, and we don't know

00:08:20.060 --> 00:08:21.020
what all the primes look like of course

00:08:21.020 --> 00:08:22.250
there's infinitely many of them but but

00:08:22.250 --> 00:08:24.500
we can give a descriptive definition of

00:08:24.500 --> 00:08:26.390
this particular family. And it turns out

00:08:26.390 --> 00:08:27.830
there's various other infinite families

00:08:27.830 --> 00:08:29.450
of simple groups which are a little more

00:08:29.450 --> 00:08:31.850
difficult to define so I won't do that

00:08:31.850 --> 00:08:33.470
here, but there's various other infinite

00:08:33.470 --> 00:08:34.610
families that arise, they don't all have

00:08:34.610 --> 00:08:37.820
to have size 'a prime number'. 
Okay so it's

00:08:37.820 --> 00:08:39.230
a little bit more complicated than sort

00:08:39.230 --> 00:08:40.910
of the factorisation of integers. What

00:08:40.910 --> 00:08:42.290
what's what's really interesting about

00:08:42.290 --> 00:08:43.460
this theorem is that we have these

00:08:43.460 --> 00:08:44.649
various infinite families that

00:08:44.649 --> 00:08:47.470
come up but there were some exceptions.

00:08:47.470 --> 00:08:49.809
Okay, so it turns out it's sort

00:08:49.809 --> 00:08:51.339
of a remarkable fact that there are 20-

00:08:51.339 --> 00:08:55.240
exactly 26 finite simple groups which do

00:08:55.240 --> 00:08:57.129
not belong to any of these infinite

00:08:57.129 --> 00:08:59.330
families.
- (They're like black sheep?)

00:08:59.330 --> 00:09:02.829
They're like black sheep, yeah, 
they they refuse to belong to any of

00:09:02.829 --> 00:09:03.999
these- any of these sort of well-known

00:09:03.999 --> 00:09:06.900
well behaved groups.
- (And there are 26 of them?)

00:09:06.900 --> 00:09:08.559
Exactly 26 of them yeah. 
So these are what are

00:09:08.559 --> 00:09:11.949
called the sporadic simple groups. Okay,

00:09:11.949 --> 00:09:13.629
so now they have a long long history so

00:09:13.629 --> 00:09:15.160
the first five of them were discovered

00:09:15.160 --> 00:09:20.110
in the 1860s - 1870s; and then the sixth

00:09:20.110 --> 00:09:21.369
one wasn't discovered until about 100

00:09:21.369 --> 00:09:23.949
years later in the 1960s. And then over

00:09:23.949 --> 00:09:26.800
the subsequent 10-15 years a further 20

00:09:26.800 --> 00:09:28.179
of these sporadic groups were discovered;

00:09:28.179 --> 00:09:29.589
and this all came out of this intensive

00:09:29.589 --> 00:09:31.029
effort going into the proof of the

00:09:31.029 --> 00:09:32.199
classification theorem, this enormous

00:09:32.199 --> 00:09:33.459
enormous project.

00:09:33.459 --> 00:09:37.980
The result is probably true and I 
